1
00:03:27,692 --> 00:03:33,692
Bad things happen everywhere.

2
00:06:55,384 --> 00:07:01,384
Escape from Tomorrow is a horror, fantasy, comedy film directed by Randy Moore.

3
00:10:23,076 --> 00:10:29,076
In a world of fake castles and anthropomorphic rodents, an epic battle begins when an unemployed
father's sanity is challenged by a chance encounter with two underage girls on holiday.

4
00:13:50,769 --> 00:13:56,769
Jim, played by Roy Abramsohn.

5
00:17:18,461 --> 00:17:24,461
Emily, played by Elena Schuber.

6
00:20:46,153 --> 00:20:52,153
Sara, played by Katelynn Rodriguez.

7
00:24:13,846 --> 00:24:19,846
Elliot, played by Jack Dalton.

8
00:27:41,538 --> 00:27:47,538
Sophie, played by Danielle Safady.

9
00:31:09,230 --> 00:31:15,230
Isabelle, played by Annet Mahendru.

10
00:34:36,923 --> 00:34:42,923
Escape from Tomorrow is a 2013 American independent horror film written
and directed by Randy Moore in his directorial debut.

11
00:38:04,615 --> 00:38:10,615
It tells the story of an unemployed father having increasingly bizarre experiences and
disturbing visions on the last day of a family vacation at the Walt Disney World Resort.

12
00:41:32,307 --> 00:41:38,307
It premiered in January at the 2013 Sundance Film Festival and was later a personal selection
of Roger Ebert, shown at his 15th annual film festival in Champaign, Illinois.

13
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
The film was a 2012 official selection of the PollyGrind Film Festival, but at the time
filmmakers were still working on some legal issues and asked that it not be screened.

14
00:48:27,692 --> 00:48:33,692
The film drew attention, because Moore had shot most of it on location at both Walt Disney World Resort and
Disneyland Park without permission from the Walt Disney Company, owner and operator of both properties.

15
00:51:55,384 --> 00:52:01,384
Due to Disney's reputation of being protective of its intellectual property, the cast and crew used guerrilla filmmaking techniques to avoid attracting
attention, such as keeping their scripts on their phones and shooting on handheld video cameras similar to those used by park visitors.

16
00:55:23,076 --> 00:55:29,076
After principal photography was complete, Moore was so determined to keep the
film a secret from Disney that he edited it in South Korea.

17
00:58:50,769 --> 00:58:56,769
Sundance similarly declined to discuss the film in detail before it was shown.

18
01:02:18,461 --> 01:02:24,461
It has been called "the ultimate guerrilla film".

19
01:05:46,153 --> 01:05:52,153
Rather than suppressing the film as Moore anticipated
would happen, Disney did not respond.

20
01:09:13,846 --> 01:09:19,846
It has been compared to the work of Roman Polanski and David Lynch.

21
01:12:41,538 --> 01:12:47,538
Although many who saw it at the Sundance Film Festival expressed strong doubts that the film would be shown to a wider audience due to the legal
issues involved and the negative depiction of the parks, the Walt Disney Company did not prevent the film from being released.

22
01:16:09,230 --> 01:16:15,230
At the time of its premiere, Disney stated that it was "aware" of the film; since then the online
supplement to Disney A to Z: The Official Encyclopedia has included an entry for the film.

23
01:19:36,923 --> 01:19:42,923
It was released simultaneously to theaters and video on-demand on October 11,
2013, through Producers Distribution Agency, a Cinetic Media company.

24
01:23:04,615 --> 01:23:10,615
It has received mixed to negative reviews, praising its visuals and
ambitious production, but criticizing its execution.

25
01:26:32,307 --> 01:26:38,307
The tone is lighthearted and dark and emotional.
